---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const references = ["Rig Veda", "Mahabharata", "Bhagavad Gita", "Shiva Purana", "Vishnu Purana"];
---
<BaseLayout title="Celestial Chariots | Sanatana Path" description="The vehicles of the gods: Indra's chariot, Surya's solar chariot, Arjuna's chariot, and Shiva's cosmic chariot." image="/images/home/home-vimana.webp" type="article">
  <div data-reading-body class="vimana-topic">
    <section class="topic-hero"><img src="/images/home/home-vimana.webp" alt="Celestial vehicle in the sky" loading="eager" decoding="async" /><div class="hero-card"><a class="back-link" href="/vimanas">Vimanas</a><p class="eyebrow">Topic 4</p><h1>Celestial Chariots</h1><strong>Episode 5 - The Vehicles of the Gods</strong><p>"Before kingdoms built roads... the gods traveled the pathways of the cosmos."</p></div></section>
    <section class="topic-layout"><article class="reading-panel">
      <p>Across the sacred texts of Sanatana Dharma, the gods rarely walk. They arrive.</p><p>Sometimes in blazing light. Sometimes on radiant chariots. Sometimes upon extraordinary beings.</p><p>Each vehicle reflects the nature of the deity. They are not merely transportation. They are symbols of cosmic power.</p>
      <h2>Indra's Golden Chariot</h2><p>When storms gathered, the king of the Devas rode into battle. His chariot flashed like lightning, driven by the divine charioteer Matali and pulled by celestial horses.</p><p>It carried Indra against powerful Asuras. The Rig Veda frequently celebrates Indra's divine chariot as an instrument of victory.</p>
      <h2>Surya's Solar Chariot</h2><p>Every sunrise tells an ancient story. The Sun does not simply rise. According to the scriptures, Surya travels across the sky in a radiant chariot.</p><p>Driven by Aruna, it is drawn by seven horses, or in some interpretations one horse with seven rays, symbolizing the seven colors of light, the seven meters of Vedic poetry, or the seven days of the week.</p>
      <h2>Arjuna's Divine Chariot</h2><p>Few chariots are more famous than the one on the battlefield of Kurukshetra. Arjuna stood ready for war, and holding the reins was Krishna Himself.</p><p>That chariot became the setting for one of humanity's greatest spiritual dialogues: the Bhagavad Gita. Its true destination was not victory. It was wisdom.</p>
      <h2>The Chariot of Shiva</h2><p>In the story of Tripura Samhara, the entire universe became Shiva's chariot. Earth formed its base. The Sun and Moon became its wheels. The Vedas became its horses. Mount Meru became its bow. Vishnu became the arrow.</p><p>This is one of the grandest symbolic visions in Hindu literature.</p>
      <h2>More Than Vehicles</h2><ul><li>Dharma</li><li>Divine authority</li><li>Cosmic balance</li><li>Spiritual progress</li></ul><p>Their journeys are as much inward as outward.</p><h2>Journeys beyond vehicles</h2><p>The gods traveled. The sages traveled. The kings traveled.</p><p>But how?</p><p>Could movement between worlds happen without roads, without wings, or even without vehicles?</p><p>The final chapter explores the mysterious idea of divine transportation.</p>
    </article><aside class="side-panel"><div class="insight-card"><p class="eyebrow">Did you know?</p><p>The Sanskrit word Ratha means chariot, but in many Upanishadic passages it also becomes a metaphor for the human body, with the soul as the passenger and the intellect as the charioteer.</p></div><details class="source-drawer"><summary><span>Source references</span><strong>{references.length}</strong></summary><ul>{references.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul></details><a class="next-card" href="/divine-transportation"><span>Next chapter</span><strong>Divine Transportation</strong><small>Continue the journey -&gt;</small></a></aside></section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout><style>
  :global(body:has(.vimana-topic)){background:light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 1), #020b10);color:light-dark(rgb(43 33 24 / 1), #fff4df)}:global(body:has(.vimana-topic)::before){display:none}.vimana-topic{min-height:100svh;padding-bottom:4rem;background:radial-gradient(circle at 78% 0%,rgb(255 191 115/.13),transparent 30rem),linear-gradient(180deg,light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 1), #020b10),light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 1), #031517) 54%,light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 1), #020b10))}.topic-hero{position:relative;width:min(1460px,calc(100% - 2rem));min-height:clamp(28rem,56svh,38rem);margin:1.25rem auto 0;overflow:hidden;border:1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.28), rgb(255 191 115/.28));border-radius:1.1rem;background:light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 1), #061117);box-shadow:inset 0 1px rgb(255 255 255/.035),0 28px 90px light-dark(rgb(91 69 38 / 0.112), rgb(0 0 0/.32))}.topic-hero img{position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover;filter:saturate(1.08) contrast(1.06) brightness(.66)}.topic-hero::after{position:absolute;inset:0;background:linear-gradient(90deg,light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.96), rgb(2 11 16/.96)),light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.58), rgb(2 11 16/.58)) 48%,light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.16), rgb(2 11 16/.16))),linear-gradient(180deg,transparent 58%,light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.62), rgb(2 11 16/.62)));content:""}.hero-card{position:relative;z-index:1;width:min(48rem,54%);margin:clamp(1.25rem,3vw,2.5rem);border:1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.34), rgb(255 191 115/.34));border-radius:1rem;padding:clamp(1.2rem,2.6vw,2rem);background:linear-gradient(145deg,light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.68), rgb(3 13 20/.68)),light-dark(rgb(247 240 226 / 0.3), rgb(3 13 20/.3)));box-shadow:inset 0 1px rgb(255 255 255/.08),0 24px 70px light-dark(rgb(91 69 38 / 0.077), rgb(0 0 0/.22));backdrop-filter:blur(20px) saturate(126%)}.back-link{display:inline-flex;width:fit-content;margin-bottom:1.2rem;border:1px solid light-dark(rgb(118 93 55 / 0.34), rgb(255 191 115/